\chapter{Creole and Cajun Personal Networks}
  \label{chap:networks}
  The associations between realizations of subject pronouns and ethnicity have made clear the general tendencies for Creole and Cajun participants here.
  Despite the regularity of these associations, however, some speakers showed exceptions to the pattern.
  Elizabeth, for instance, identified as a White Cajun but produced exclusively the Creole-marked \lexi{li} for 3sg.F.
  Likewise, James identified as a Black Creole but produced exclusively the Cajun-marked \lexi{ils} for 3pl.
  To get a more fine-grained understanding of such situations, social network analysis has proven effective in previous studies.

  Prototypically, social network analysis involves elaborating who is connected to whom, although it can also involve elaborating who is connected to what or what is connected to what.\footnote{
    Social network analysis is in fact an area of the more general network analysis, which is itself simply the applied version of the branch of mathematics called graph theory \parencite{barnes_graph_1983}.
  }
  In doing so, one obtains a direct view of quantifiable social structure.
  Social positions become more observable and less inferred, and qualities of individuals' social worlds can be measured beyond broad demographic information.
  Just as how \textcite{milroy_language_1987} was famously able to explain why two speakers with whom she worked spoke differently despite sharing several broad social characteristics by looking at how their social networks differed (pp.~131-134), this chapter aims at using personal social networks to better understand the variation found in Creoles' and Cajuns' subject pronoun systems.
  More specifically, the goal is to measure ethnic homophily in speakers' networks to see if those homophily values are related to the overall subject pronoun tendencies and especially to the linguistic behavior of those who do not follow the tendencies uncovered in Chapter \ref{chap:pronoun_variation}.
  In the process of carrying out this analysis, a better understanding of participants' social structures is also uncovered.

  \section{Social network analysis in sociolinguistics}
    \label{sec:sna_sociolinguistics}
    The fundamental focus on interaction in social network analysis has been understood as important in linguistics as a predictor of how one will speak long before the methods were implemented into the field.
    Quite early on, for example, \textcite{bloomfield_language_1933} argued that those who are in frequent contact would likely end up speaking similarly (pp.~46-47).
    The idea that social networks can be explanatory for individuals' linguistic behavior has been brought up repeatedly, including when racialized barriers are crossed \parencite{sweetland_unexpected_2002}.
    The social network approach to analyzing social structure is appealing enough that it has even been invoked much more recently when no network measures are actually implemented \parencite{hoffman_ethnolects_2010, sneller_gritty_2024}.

    What makes social network analysis so appealing in sociolinguistics is that its quantificational nature fits neatly into traditional variationist methods.
    In particular, it is useful for examining the spread (\foreign{i.e.}, diffusion) of linguistic features \parencite[pp.~262-263]{dodsworth_speech_2014}.
    Diffusion was in fact a central focus early on in sociology, where social network analysis was developed, especially when considering how the closeness of connections (\foreign{i.e.}, the strength of ties) impacts the spread of information \parencite{granovetter_strength_1973, milgram_small-world_1967}.
    The role of race in this diffusion was also regularly present \parencite{korte_acquaintance_1970}.
    Early studies of language change posited that diffusion between groups occurs across strong ties or specifically when individuals have many ties both in and out of a group (\citeauthor{downes_language_1984}, \citeyear[p.~155]{downes_language_1984}; \citeauthor{labov_social_1980}, \citeyear[p.~261]{labov_social_1980}), contrary to arguments for the mechanics of diffusion that had been made in sociology.
    The network data that is needed to examine diffusion directly is quite difficult to collect, however, making linguistics studies that would resolve the diffusion question few and far between.

    While \textcite{milroy_language_1987} was not the first sociolinguist to actively attempt to bring social network analysis into the field from sociology -- that honor arguably goes to \textcite{gal_language_1979} -- her work has clearly been the most influential in terms of methodology.
    The major theoretical contribution of her study was the finding that a speaker's lack of integration into a community (\foreign{i.e.}, their lack of centrality) can explain their linguistic difference from that community when broad demographic categories would otherwise predict that they would reproduce the community norms.
    \textcite{milroy_language_1987} measured centrality with a 5-point index where points were tabulated for factors such as working with two people in the community or spending leisure time with those people (pp.~139/141-142).
    Numerous such indices have been used since then in sociolinguistics with similar results \parencite{cheshire_present_1978, lippi-green_social_1989}.
    Some of this work has even focused on race \parencite{ash_linguistic_1986, edwards_sociolinguistic_1992}.
    In the case of \textcite{edwards_sociolinguistic_1992}, one of the index questions posed to his Black participants was whether they often interacted with White people.
    The results of his analysis thus suggested that a lack of interaction between people of different racialized groups leads to differences in speech patterns just as a lack of interaction between any two people may do.
    
    Importantly, though, these indices are not generalizable in the sense of being equally applicable to all datasets.
    When \textcite{feagin_competing_1986} tried to apply Milroy's index as-is, the results were quite difficult to interpret.
    As \textcite{lippi-green_social_1989} argued, these indices are idiosyncratic and therefore need to be adapted to each community (p.~218).
    The sort of broad structural data required to employ generalizable centrality measures from sociology is rare in sociolinguistics outside of studies of online networks \parencite{kim_sociolinguistic_2014, mcneill_lol_2018, paolillo_virtual_1999}, making indices a necessary compromise.

    Another social network analysis that is more easily carried out and has more often dealt with ethnicity and race is personal network analysis, which centers on particular individuals (\foreign{i.e.}, egos) and elaborates only the ties to other individuals who are connected to these egos at some defined distance (\foreign{i.e.}, alters) without concerning oneself with the egos' positions in the overall community (\citeauthor{bott_family_1971}, \citeyear[p.~250]{bott_family_1971}; \citeauthor{perry_egocentric_2018}, \citeyear[p.~6]{perry_egocentric_2018}).
    Such analyses involve examining the characteristics of alters, sometimes relative to the characteristics of egos.
    Perhaps the most common way this is done is by looking at homophily, that is the likeness between egos and their alters on some characteristic \parencite[p.~23]{lazarsfeld_friendship_1954}.
    In sociolinguistics, some of the most robust network analyses have taken this approach.
    For example, \textcite{gal_language_1979} found that the composite peasantness\footnote{
      Peasantness was the term \textcite{gal_language_1979} used because her participants also used it (pp.~45-46).
      She measured peasantness with an index based on participants' lifestyles and symbolic factors of peasantness that applied to them (pp.~137-139).
    } of participants' personal network alters was more predictive of their language choices than their own peasantness, while \textcite{stoessel_investigating_2002} found that immigrants were more likely to maintain their heritage language if they had more speakers of that language in their personal networks and if those speakers were in close proximity physically.

    The ethnic homophily of one's network has been found to influence one's language use in several ways, one of which is in language choice.
    This has especially been a focus of studies of immigrant groups and their offspring.
    In England, members of Chinese families were found to choose Chinese with interlocutors when their personal networks were more Chinese \parencite{li_two-step_2000}, and members of Punjabi families were also more likely to use Punjabi or Urdu when their personal networks included more Punjabis (\citeauthor{reynolds_punjabiurdu_2002}, \citeyear{reynolds_punjabiurdu_2002}; \citeauthor{sharma_scalar_2017}, \citeyear[p.~406]{sharma_scalar_2017}).
    Similar results were found in Norway where Filipino immigrants with higher ethnic homophily in their personal networks were more likely to use Filipino languages with their children \parencite{lanza_tell_2007}.
    This pattern has also held when looking at colonial rather than immigrant contexts.
    For example, in the small Pacific country of Palau, participants with more Japanese alters were found to be more likely to use Japanese, a former colonial language \parencite{matsumoto_role_2010}.
    This colonial context is somewhat applicable to the situation in Louisiana with the major caveat that the break with Japan occurred more recently, during WWII, than Louisiana's break with France, and so some of the intermarried families had a parent who returned to Japan at that time and only came back later.
    Migratory patterns like those in Palau could have occurred in Louisiana shortly after the Louisiana Purchase, but it is unlikely that many families in the state still hold such close ties with France or any other French-speaking nation outside of Louisiana today.

    Ethnic homophily has also been seen as important for variation within language, several studies of which have examined groups in New York City.
    Although these two samples were small, the Irish immigrants who maintained the Irish variant of English /t/ were those who had more Irish alters in their networks \parencite{kirke_when_2005}, and Chinese-Americans with more ethnic homophily in their networks did not use the local New York variant of English /ɔ/ that those with low ethnic homophily did use \parencite{wong_new_2010}.
    However, in the UK, there was a significant effect on English (ing) relative to the proportion of Polish alters in the networks of Polish immigrants in Edinburgh but not of Polish immigrants in London \parencite{schleef_teenagers_2011}.
    It would seem that while ethnic homophily in one's network tends to be a meaningful predictor of language variation, other circumstances can override the effect.
    This is further supported by the finding that higher ``Asianness'' in the personal networks of Punjabis west of London led to greater use of [ʈ] for English /t/ only in the middle generation of those who were examined \parencite[pp.~418-419]{sharma_cognitive_2011}.
    In comparing each of these cases to the situation in Louisiana, caution needs to be taken, though.
    Much like the different stages of post-colonialism in which Palau and Louisiana find themselves, the studies mentioned here all involve relatively recent immigration and the navigation of identity around that fact, whereas speakers of F\&C in Louisiana have no need to find a balance between local norms and their heritage as their heritage in fact represents the local norm.

    A social context that is perhaps more congruent with that of Creoles and Cajuns in Louisiana is that of Black and White Americans as these categories are generally superimposed over the local categories.
    Like the studies just mentioned, however, the results for studies of race in the US have not shown consistent effects for personal network racial homophily.
    \textcite{labov_facto_1986}, for instance, looked at a slightly extended personal network for Eddie, a Black man in North Philadelphia, and concluded that the morphological patterns of those in his network were not entirely explainable by the make-up of racialized identities in the network because the life histories of racialized in-group members were not all the same.
    Likewise, a longitudinal study of African American youths in Chapel Hill, North Carolina showed that the number of African American social contacts of participants simply did not have a significant effect on the rate of usage of African American English features \parencite{van_hofwegen_coming_2010}.
    This result, again, could perhaps be explainable through other social factors superseding the potential effects of network homophily.

    Other studies of race in the US, in contrast, have shown clear effects for racial homophily.
    African Americans in Memphis who had higher rates of racial homophily in their networks were found to be more involved in the Southern Vowel Shift than those with networks that have greater diversity in racialized identities \parencite{fridland_network_2003}.
    Unfortunately, this sample was limited to 10 participants, so no statistical analyses were used to establish this pattern.
    A larger study, however, was conducted in Philadelphia, just as \citeauthor{labov_facto_1986}'s (\citeyear{labov_facto_1986}) work was, and found that Black speakers with more racially homophilous networks used more linguistic features associated with Black speakers than others.
    Likewise, White speakers in the data with little contact with Black speakers used fewer Black linguistic features, although White participants constituted a minority of the sample \parencite{ash_linguistic_1986}.
    This has perhaps been the most transparent and robust study of the impact of homophily on the language use of racialized groups as the sample included 28 speakers for whom homophily was calculated rather than inferred and whose homophily rates ranged from very high to very low.
    Where racialized social systems are in place, in fact, one should probably not expect to find low racial homophily in the first place.
    If participants who have low racial homophily in their personal networks are outliers, it is not surprising that studies of language variation are sometimes unable to show significant effects for the amount of racial homophily given the very small range in that amount, but this does not necessarily mean that racial homophily is not an important factor.

    In Louisiana specifically, while there is a long and continuing history of racial segregation that has been examined at the community level (see Section \ref{sec:united_states}), there is a dearth of examinations of what personal networks look like along ethnic or racialized lines whether this means dealing with Creoles and Cajuns or Black and White people.
    In sociolinguistics, this gap is especially true.
    While sociolinguistic ethnographic work on Creoles has taken place in Louisiana \parencite{wendte_tale_2020}, quantitative network analyses have been absent.
    Only one study, outside of sociolinguistics, has addressed how the make-up of racialized identities in personal networks relates to Creoles, in this case in terms of how they view their Creole identities.
    It was found that those Creoles of color who identified as singular Black had mostly Black friends, whereas those who identified as border Creole (\foreign{i.e.}, a stable mixed racialized identity) had mostly White friends, and those who identified as protean (\foreign{i.e.}, shifting between racialized identities) had a relatively even mix of racialized identities in their friend groups \parencite[pp.~45/48]{susberry_racial_2004}.

    The current study aims to add to this picture by looking not at the Black and White composition of personal networks but the Creole and Cajun composition of the personal networks of individuals who self-identify as Creole or Cajun themselves and how this impacts language use.
    Specifically, the primary research question is the following:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item[RQ2:] How does the homophily or lack thereof in the ethnic make-up of personal networks among F\&C speakers in South Louisiana relate to variation in subject pronoun usage?
    \end{itemize}
    Along the way, useful descriptive details about the social structures surrounding Creoles and Cajuns will be presented.

  \section{Methods}
    \label{sec:network_methods}
    In order to examine ethnic homophily among Creoles and Cajuns, personal network data was collected for each participant.
    This presented a few challenges related to how to ask for alters and how to gather information on those alters, all of which will be discussed in section \ref{sec:network_collection}.
    Other than the regression model that was fit to determine the effect of network ethnic homophily on subject pronoun usage, the quantitative analyses carried out consisted of relatively straightforward descriptive statistics.
    There were, however, important decisions made about how to prepare the data for calculating those statistics which will be discussed in section \ref{sec:network_analyses}.

    \subsection{Data collection}
      \label{sec:network_collection}
      The data used for the analysis of personal networks came from the same participants as the analysis of subject pronoun variation.
      Network questions were asked during the same interviews, and almost all participants responded to these questions.
      The two exceptions were Rachel, a Creole, and Errol, a Cajun.
      The target for interview lengths was an hour with network questions coming last in most cases.\footnote{
        Technically, the transcription task was left for last but was skipped for most participants.
      } This duration has been cited as leading to interviewer fatigue effects but not interviewee fatigue effects \parencite{paik_social_2013}.
      Indeed, my participants were happy to keep talking even during the longest interviews with the only exception being Alice, and given that there was only one interviewer, fatigue would likely have been similar in each case.
      Instead, in the case of Rachel, the interview took place during a break in her work to which she had to return after about \Sexpr{round((as.numeric(hms(participants[participants$Name == "Rachel Chenevert", "Total Length Time"])))/60)} minutes, short of the time when network questions would have been asked.
      In the case of Errol, he simply felt uncomfortable providing names even when it was explained to him that he could give just initials or given names.

      Network data was therefore collected for the remaining \Sexpr{length(table(networks$Name))} participants.
      This task was carried out in F\&C in order to continue collecting language data.
      Two name generator questions were posed to each participant as well as two name interpreter questions to elicit information about each alter who was named.
      The goal of name generator questions is essentially to determine with whom participants interact, but of course many forms of interaction exist, and so name generator questions must be designed to elicit the desired alter types.
      In this case, two name generators were used as this has been found to be more effective for building a complete picture of personal networks, depending on the network measures in which one is interested, than relying on a lower-burden, single name generator \parencite{marin_simplifying_2007}.
      
      The first name generator question is the important matters question used for the General Social Survey (GSS).
      Not only has this generator been thoroughly investigated \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][]{burt_whats_2012, ruan_content_1998}, but it is also one of the two questions used in the multiple generator approach that \textcite{marin_simplifying_2007} examined.
      The question, along with the F\&C version used for this study, is reproduced here as Sentence \ref{sent:name_generator_GSS}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item From time to time, most people discuss important matters with other people.
              Looking back over the last six months -- that would be back to last August -- who are the people with whom you discussed an important personal matter? \parencite[p.~331]{burt_network_1984}\label{sent:name_generator_GSS}
        \item[] \longtrans{Tous les temps en temps, le monde parle pour les affaires importantes avec d’autre monde.
              Si tu peux jongler pour les six derniers mois, qui sont les individus avec qui toi tu parles pour les affaires qui te sont importantes?}
      \end{enumerate}
      While the translation was literally comprehensible for participants on the surface, it did sometimes need to be explained further so that participants could understand the sort of people I was asking them to name.
      The most common confusion was what was meant by ``important personal matters,'' as this was sometimes interpreted as being business matters or philosophical matters, leading to the naming of people such as lawyers or those with whom they discuss topics such as politics.
      Another confusion, however, was whether the question was about discussing important matters specifically in F\&C rather than in any language, the latter being the intent.

      This name generator was originally found to yield a mean of 3.01 alters with a $SD$ of 1.77 \parencite[p.~125]{marsden_core_1987}, which is in line with those generated in the current study (mean $\approx$ \Sexpr{round(mean(table(networks[networks$`Alter Type` == "Core", "Name"])), 2)}, $SD \approx$ \Sexpr{round(sd(table(networks[networks$`Alter Type` == "Core", "Name"])), 2)}).
      These numbers for the important matters question may appear rather small, but this has been found to be one of the most robust questions for getting good results \parencite[p.~163]{marin_simplifying_2007}.
      Alters named in this way were coded as core alters.
      
      The second name generator question, as shown in Sentence \ref{sent:name_generator_french}, was aimed at generating what were coded as non-core alters.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Qui sont les personnes avec qui tu parles souvent français?\label{sent:name_generator_french}
        \item[] \longtrans{Who are the people with whom you often speak French?}
      \end{enumerate}
      The question focuses specifically on the use of F\&C to ensure that personal networks were not wholly made up of alters with whom participants only speak English as such networks would not provide any means of drawing connections between participants' subject pronoun usage in F\&C and their personal networks.
      Despite most participants referring to their language as \lexi{français} \gloss{French}, this question was at times extended to say \foreign{français ou créole} \gloss{French or Creole} for fear that participants might name only speakers of a single specific language variety otherwise.
      It was also often necessary to clarify that the question was asking them to name people with whom they speak F\&C at all, not necessarily those with whom they speak exclusively F\&C, although frequent, primarily F\&C-using interlocutors were in fact of greater interest than others.
      This question resulted in a mean of \Sexpr{round(mean(table(networks[networks$`Alter Type` == "Non-Core", "Name"])), 2)} more named alters ($SD \approx$ \Sexpr{round(sd(table(networks[networks$`Alter Type` == "Non-Core", "Name"])), 2)}), bringing the total to a mean of \Sexpr{round(mean(table(networks$Name)), 2)} ($SD \approx$ \Sexpr{round(sd(table(networks$Name)), 2)}).
      These numbers themselves highlight that obtaining a fixed number of alters was not the goal.
      While aiming for a fixed number of alters may help for comparability between networks, it can also lead to overlooking important ties or including unimportant ties \parencite[p.~345]{sharma_language_2020}.

      With the list of alters generated, participants were asked two name interpreter questions immediately afterward to gather more data on characteristics of alters.
      One of these questions, reproduced in Sentence \ref{sent:interpret_ethnicity}, constituted a type of proxy report \parencite[p.~87]{cobb_answering_2018}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Pour chaque personne sur cette liste, quoi c’est son éthnicité, c’est-à-dire son \foreign{ethnicity}?\label{sent:interpret_ethnicity}
        \item[] \longtrans{For each person on this list, what is their ethnicity, that is to say their ethnicity?}
      \end{enumerate}
      It has been suggested that these questions be asked during a follow-up meeting with participants as this lowers interviewee fatigue and increases accuracy \parencite[p.~245]{stark_collecting_2018}, but for the current study, the lack of any sign of fatigue from participants meant that the greater risk would have been attempting to set up follow-up meetings.
      Not only would participants perhaps lose their enthusiasm for partaking in the interim, but follow-ups would increase both the time needed to complete data collection and the cost of data collection, two factors for which resources were limited.

      It is reasonable to also doubt the accuracy of proxy reports in general.
      Previous work has found open-ended questions to yield the most highly accurate responses \parencite{cobb_proxy_2018}, which is in fact the sort of name interpreter question that Sentence \ref{sent:interpret_ethnicity} represents.
      Additionally, as has previously been observed, even when accuracy in terms of agreement between the respondent and the people they are describing is low, what is often more important is the respondent's perception of alters \parencite[p.~277]{cornwell_survey_2014}.
      While it would have been beneficial for the ethnicity question to know what the alters themselves would say, a participant perceiving alters as having one ethnicity or another makes up one half of the process of negotiating one's ethnic identity with others of the relevant group, the first being that one sees oneself as a member of an ethnic group and the second being that others in the group accept one as a member of the group (\citeauthor{brubaker_ethnicity_2002}, \citeyear{brubaker_ethnicity_2002}; \citeauthor{giles_ethnicity_1979}, \citeyear[pp.~252-253]{giles_ethnicity_1979}; \citeauthor{le_page_acts_1985}, \citeyear[p.~2]{le_page_acts_1985}; \citeauthor{moerman_ethnic_1965}, \citeyear{moerman_ethnic_1965}).
      Essentially, if a Cajun describes an alter as Cajun, that alter is being accepted as a Cajun, and indeed, as will be discussed in section \ref{sec:network_analyses}, agreement was high between participants when they were describing the ethnicities of shared alters.

      At times, participants needed some clarification as to what exactly they were being asked for the ethnicity question.
      This could take the form of misunderstanding whether they were being asked if they know what an alter would say rather than just what they themselves would say about the alter.
      The question was therefore often followed up with an explanation that their answer did not have to be exactly what their alter would say.
      Another form of clarification that was sometimes needed was what was meant by the term \lexi{ethnicity}.
      The word was repeated in English in the original question as \lexi{ethnicité} is not a widely used word in the community, but examples were also provided.
      Typically, these examples included Cajun, Creole, American, Indian, and Latino, all of which was generally sufficient as an explanation.

      The second name interpreter question, reproduced in Sentence \ref{sent:interpret_french}, aimed at obtaining the proportion of French used in discourse with each alter.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Pour chaque personne sur cette liste, combien souvent tu parles français avec?\label{sent:interpret_french}
        \item[] \longtrans{For each person on this list, how often do you speak French with them?}
      \end{enumerate}
      This question was asked for each alter, non-core and core alike, as being named as a core alter (\foreign{i.e.}, as someone with whom a participant would discuss important matters) was not mutually exclusive with the possibility of speaking F\&C with that person, as well.
      There was generally no confusion related to this question.

    \subsection{Analyses}
      \label{sec:network_analyses}
      Generally, the analyses of participants' personal networks that were undertaken involved straightforward descriptive statistics.
      One important exception was the statistic used to quantify homophily, external-internal (EI) homophily \parencite{lizardo_social_2020}, given in Equation \ref{eq:ei_homophily}.
      \begin{enumerate}[label=\roman*., ref=\roman*, resume=equations]
        \item EI homophily $= \frac{\text{External} - \text{Internal}}{\text{External} + \text{Internal}}$\label{eq:ei_homophily}
      \end{enumerate}
      EI homophily was calculated for each participant's personal social network.
      The external component is the count of all out-group alters, whereas the internal component is the count of all in-group alters.
      In the present context, an in-group alter would be one who has the same ethnicity as ego (\foreign{i.e.}, the participant), and an out-group alter is any other alter.
      The statistic produces values from -1 to 1 where -1 represents perfect homophily and 1 the complete absence of homophily, or in other words, -1 represents a participant whose alters all have the same ethnicity as the participant and 1 represents a participant whose alters all have different ethnicities from the participant.

      The more important discussion in terms of analyses, however, is the way in which the ethnicities of alters were coded both from the perspective of personal networks and from the perspective of the whole network.
      When analyzing personal networks, it was necessary to decide how to code alters who were given multiple or compound ethnicities by participants.
      Multiple ethnicities refers to, for example, when an alter is described as both African American and Creole.
      Compound ethnicities, on the other hand, included alters who were given labels that combined multiple categories into a single, more nuanced category such as Creole-African American or Americanized Cajun.
      Maintaining these multiple and compound ethnicities as-is, while interesting to discuss and take account of qualitatively, also risked overlooking what are essentially homophilous relationships.
      When a Cajun participant describes an alter as an ``Americanized Cajun'', it is not likely that they see that alter as being completely ethnically distinct from themselves.
      The use of a qualifier does indeed suggest that the participant does not see this alter as being ethnically identical, but to deny the ethnic connection altogether would also be inaccurate.
      With that in mind, all multiple and compound ethnicities that included Creole were collapsed to simply Creole, and those that included Cajun were collapsed to simply Cajun.
      These changes are summarized in Table \ref{tab:collapsed_alter_ethnicities}, which also demonstrates that these cases made up a relatively small number of the total of \Sexpr{nrow(networks)} alters named.
      Homophily values before and after this collapsing were both calculated, as will be seen in Table \ref{tab:homophily_participants}, but the values derived from the collapsed alter ethnicities were those that were used for the primary statistical analyses.

      \begin{table}[tbhp]
        \caption{Collapsed alter ethnicities}
        \label{tab:collapsed_alter_ethnicities}
        \centering
        \begin{tabular}{r r @{ $\to$ } l}
          Count & Given & Coded \\
          \hline
          \Sexpr{nrow(networksUncollapsedEth[networksUncollapsedEth$`Alter Ethnicity` == "African American/Creole",])} & African American/Creole   & Creole \\
          \Sexpr{nrow(networksUncollapsedEth[networksUncollapsedEth$`Alter Ethnicity` == "African/Creole",])}          & African/Creole            & Creole \\
          \Sexpr{nrow(networksUncollapsedEth[networksUncollapsedEth$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Creole-African American",])} & Creole-African American   & Creole \\
          \Sexpr{nrow(networksUncollapsedEth[networksUncollapsedEth$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Creole/French",])}           & Creole/French             & Creole \\
          \Sexpr{nrow(networksUncollapsedEth[networksUncollapsedEth$`Alter Ethnicity` == "White Creole",])}            & White-Creole              & Creole \\
          \Sexpr{nrow(networksUncollapsedEth[networksUncollapsedEth$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Americanized Cajun",])}      & Americanized-Cajun        & Cajun \\
          \Sexpr{nrow(networksUncollapsedEth[networksUncollapsedEth$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Cajun-American",])}          & Cajun-American            & Cajun \\
          \multicolumn{3}{c}{} \\
        \end{tabular}

        {\footnotesize
        / $\to$ multiple ethnicities; - $\to$ compound ethnicity
        }
      \end{table}

      One special case remained which was an alter residing in Canada who was described by Steven as Acadian.
      As was discussed in Section \ref{sec:cajun_history}, Acadians are central to how Cajuns understand their own ethnic origin, so much so that some Louisianians will still use Acadian as a synonym for Cajun such as in the title of the annual Lafayette music festival, \foreign{Festivals Acadiens et Créoles}.
      There is therefore an argument for recoding this alter as Cajun, but this was not done because several other alters named by Steven were described as Cajun, suggesting that he made a distinction between these two labels.

      While no formal whole network analyses were carried out in this study, it was informative to elaborate the alters who formed bridges between participants who were coincidentally identified.
      For these \Sexpr{length(levels(networkSharedAlters$Alter))} alters who were named by more than one participant, a choice needed to be made about the ethnicity to assign to them when representing them in the whole network where each individual is represented by one node as it is not a given that one alter named by two participants will be assigned the same ethnicity by both participants.
      Indeed, for exactly half of these alters, there was a mismatch in ethnicities assigned to them, whereas there was complete agreement for the other half.
      Notably, one of these latter alters was also a participant, Cynthia, whose ethnic self-identification was identical to what five others assigned to her.
      Two other shared alters were also participants, Lucille and Richard, and so the ethnicities assigned to them in the whole network were those which they used as their self-identifications as this matched at least one of their ethnic assignments given to them by participants who named them as alters.
      
      There were three other shared alters, all non-participants, who had more than one ethnicity assigned to them by participants.
      One of these, Bruce Rivero, was described once as Cajun and one participant elected to not assign an ethnicity to him.
      He was thus coded as Cajun.
      For the other two, David Roche and Jacqueline Newchurch, the assigned ethnicities were Creole and African American for the former and Cajun and White for the latter.
      In accord with how such cases were treated when a single participant gave an alter more than one ethnicity, Creole and Cajun were assigned to these two, respectively.
      This choice was further supported for Jacqueline as she was described as Cajun four times and as White only once.

  \section{Results}
    \label{sec:network_results}
    The primary question being asked in this chapter is if the degree of ethnic homophily in Creole and Cajun participants' personal networks predicts subject pronoun variation.
    The first half of that question requires examining the ethnic make-up of participants' networks.
    A broad overview of the whole network as seen in Figure \ref{fig:network_whole}, to the extent that personal networks are interconnected, intuitively suggests that ethnic homophily is high.
    The figure is made up of a number of star graphs where the center node of each, ego, is a participant and all others are alters of participants.
    Almost all of these star graphs are made up of alters who are all of the same ethnicity as ego.
    Where alters are not primarily of the same ethnicity, they are generally of ethnicities other than Cajun for Creoles or Creole for Cajuns.
    In other words, no self-identified Cajun had a personal social network containing a significant number of Creoles and no self-identified Creole had a personal social network containing a significant number of Cajuns.
    It is also clear that some participants are more connected to the larger network than others.
    Those who are more connected are almost all Cajuns with the exception of the Creoles Owen and James.

    \begin{figure}[tbh]
      \centering
      <<fig.height = 0.6 * textheight>>=
      networkWholePlot()
      @
      \caption{All participants and alters}
      \label{fig:network_whole}
    \end{figure}

    These are good first indications that Creole and Cajun participants not only have ethnically homophilous personal networks but are also ethnically segregated, but as no attempt was made here to elaborate the whole community network, no whole network quantitative measures can be used with reliability to further establish that these indications are accurate.
    Participants were always told that they could provide just first names or initials of alters, meaning only those named alters for whom participants voluntarily gave family names could be connected between personal networks in the first place.
    The fact then that the network shows connections between personal star networks at all is therefore completely accidental.
    Snowball sampling would naturally lead to connections between participants, and while snowball sampling was not avoided, very few participants were obtained this way, those being Brian, Randal, and Arthur.
    Notably, neither Randal nor Arthur named Oliver G., the participant who suggested their participation in the first place, nor \foreign{vice versa}.
    Additionally, some other participants were linked because they were either interviewed together -- Lucille and Cynthia as well as Tracy and Florence -- or because they frequented locations where participants were recruited, meaning that not all of the bridges in the network were participants in the study.
    Thus, while whole network analyses are inappropriate here due to the only coincidental nature of the network having bridges at all, personal network analyses can help provide some quantitative answers to the characteristics of participants' social worlds and ultimately how those relate to subject pronoun variation.

    \subsection{Personal networks}
      \label{sec:network_results_personal}
      Looking more quantitatively at personal networks, there is a relatively clear pattern of networks being ethnically homophilous with a few caveats.
      Table \ref{tab:homophily_participants} shows the EI homophily statistic for each participant, arranged by ethnicity.
      The mean homophily and median homophily for the study overall were \Sexpr{Round(mean(participants[participants$Name != "Errol Stoufle" & participants$Name != "Rachel Chenevert", "Network Ethnic Homophily"]))} and \Sexpr{Round(median(participants[participants$Name != "Errol Stoufle" & participants$Name != "Rachel Chenevert", "Network Ethnic Homophily"]))}, respectively, with a standard deviation of \Sexpr{Round(sd(participants[participants$Name != "Errol Stoufle" & participants$Name != "Rachel Chenevert", "Network Ethnic Homophily"]))}.
      These values being below zero indicates that, on average, these personal networks show more ethnic homophily than not.
      However, when grouping by ethnicity, Creoles have a mean slightly above zero (mean $\approx$ \Sexpr{Round(mean(participants[participants$Ethnicity == "Creole" & participants$Name != "Rachel Chenevert", "Network Ethnic Homophily"]))}, $SD \approx$ \Sexpr{Round(sd(participants[participants$Ethnicity == "Creole" & participants$Name != "Rachel Chenevert", "Network Ethnic Homophily"]))}) whereas the mean for Cajuns remains below zero (mean $\approx$ \Sexpr{Round(mean(participants[participants$Ethnicity == "Cajun" & participants$Name != "Errol Stoufle", "Network Ethnic Homophily"]))}, $SD \approx$ \Sexpr{Round(sd(participants[participants$Ethnicity == "Cajun" & participants$Name != "Errol Stoufle", "Network Ethnic Homophily"]))}).
      This difference is not statistically significant ($t$-test for difference in means: $P <$ \Sexpr{Ceiling(homophByEthnicGroupTtest$p.value)}), though it nearly is.
      It might be tempting to conclude that this characteristic of the networks of Creoles and Cajuns differs between them, but a deeper look at the individuals who contradict the overall trend helps to explain why that is not the case.
      
