      The final two participants whose personal networks showed little ethnic homophily were Deborah and Gene.
      These two, both Creoles, had particularly unique network make-ups.
      For Deborah, all of her alters were core alters except one, and all of those were described as American except the non-core alter who was described as French.
      This is perhaps born out of Deborah's own rather idiosyncratic interpretation of Creole.
      She identified as a Creole and also as White, something that she explained was unique for people from the town of Creole in southwest Louisiana where she grew up.
      She also expressed during her interview, in rather vague terms, a general distaste for the term \lexi{Cajun}.
      It is thus possible that she used \lexi{American} as a default term for anyone who might be described by others as a Creole or a Cajun.

      Gene presented an even more radical picture.
      He self-identified as a Creole but could not choose a descriptor for his racialized identity, explaining that others kept changing what they would call his people over time.
      The changing terms that he used were all for broader American racialized categories such Black or Colored.
      He therefore extended this indeterminacy to the alters whom he named, all of whom were core alters, giving no answer for their ethnicities save for one individual whom he described as Creole/French.
      While this technically leads to a complete lack of ethnic homophily mathematically, like Tracy and Owen above, it is relatively clear that Gene did in fact share a racialized broad US identity with the core members of his personal network that is also associated with the Creole social category.

      There therefore appears to be substantial social distance between Creoles and Cajuns in the participants' social worlds.
      Even the few instances where Cajuns named alters in their networks and explicitly described them as Creole do not constitute very good evidence of a reduction in the social distance between the two groups, notably because it was only Cajuns who named Creoles and never Creoles who named Cajuns save for James' indirect naming of a Cajun who he classified instead as White.
      Those who did so included Elizabeth and Steven, who both named two Creoles, as well as Randal, Austin, and Oliver L., who each named one Creole.
      None of the Creoles named by any participant were part of their set of core alters, meaning these were examples of people with whom these speakers suggested they might speak F\&C from day to day and nothing more.
      In some cases, it also seemed that participants were consciously striving to make their personal networks appear more diverse than they actually were, something that Creole participants did not seem to feel the need to do.
      For instance, Oliver L. gave an individual who would come to do work on his property who had to be listed simply as ``tractor driver'' because he could not remember the gentleman's name.
      Likewise, Steven named an individual whom he could only describe as a plumber due to forgetting his name.
      He did also name another Creole whom he called Toots, a nickname, but again did not know this individual's real name despite his visible attempts to remember.
      
      That is not to say that all Creoles named by Cajuns appeared to be brought up to specifically diversify their networks rather than simply because they were the first individuals to come to mind.
      Some other Creoles who were more readily named, like those named by Elizabeth and Randal, were acquaintances they made through volunteer work, although again this highlights the non-core nature of these alters.
      For Austin, the Creole he named was even more immediately accessible in his thoughts as he was the first person named after asking Austin with whom he speaks F\&C from day to day.
      The Creole, Wayne, was one of Austin's fellow graduate students who in fact did not meet Austin's own definition of Creole, those being Black Louisiana francophones, a definition which he acknowledged was not congruent with who might self-identify as Creole in other parts of Louisiana.
      Indeed, Austin described Wayne as a White Creole due to his mother's family name and their origin in Avoyelles Parish where that family would have been considered Creole by the other locals.
      This scenario, while clearly not a robust finding, perhaps implies that the social distance between Cajuns and White Creoles is not necessarily the same as the social distance between Cajuns and Black Creoles.

      \begin{figure}[tbhp]
        \centering
        <<fig.height = 0.25 * textheight>>=
        plotHomophily
        @
        \caption{Distributions of network ethnic homophily by ethnicity}
        \label{fig:homophily_boxplots}

        \centering
        <<fig.height = 0.25 * textheight>>=
        plotHomophilyExploratory
        @
        \caption[Distributions of network ethnic homophily by ethnicity after recoding alters]{Distributions of network ethnic homophily by ethnicity after recoding alters for Gene, Owen, and Tracy}
        \label{fig:homophily_exploratory_boxplots}

        \centering
        <<fig.height = 0.25 * textheight>>=
        plotHomophilyGss
        @
        \caption[Distributions of network racial homophily by racialized category from GSS data]{Distributions of network racial homophily by racialized category from GSS data for Louisiana, Arkansas, Texas, and Oklahoma in 2004}
        \label{fig:homophily_gss_boxplots}
      \end{figure}

      To help provide greater context to the range of homophily indices, it is instructive to compare the primary values with the values derived from recoding Gene, Owen, and Tracy's alters as well as the values derived from the region in general.
      In terms of recoding Gene, Owen, and Tracy, their alters who were described as having no racialized identity, as African American, and as ``of color'' were respectively recoded as Creole given that these alters may have very well shared that identity with these participants.\footnote{
        One could possibly make the argument that Deborah's alters could be recoded, as well, but there was little indication of whether the term \lexi{American} might be referring to only Cajuns or only Creoles, in contrast to the others' alters.
      }
      Not only have Creoles consistently been described in terms of broader US racialized categories, more of which will be seen in the next chapter, but Gene, Owen, and Tracy's own self-identifications recreated the mappings used for these recodings.
      In other words, Gene self-identified as Creole and as having no racialized identity and assigned no ethnicity\footnote{
        He explained that he could not assign ethnicities to them for the same reason that he could not name his own racialized identity.
      } to his alters, Owen self-identified as Creole and as African American/Black and assigned African American as the ethnicity of all of his alters, and finally Tracy self-identified as Creole and as Black and assigned ``of color'' as the ethnicity of all of his alters.
      In terms of the region in general, data was taken from the GSS for White and Black respondents from the last year that social network questions were asked, 2004.
      Public data from the GSS cannot be constrained more narrowly to the parishes examined here nor even to just Louisiana, so this data includes Louisiana, Arkansas, Texas, and Oklahoma.
      The results are shown in the boxplots of Figures \ref{fig:homophily_boxplots}, \ref{fig:homophily_exploratory_boxplots}, and \ref{fig:homophily_gss_boxplots}.
      
      The recoding that produced Figure \ref{fig:homophily_exploratory_boxplots} not only greatly shifted the Creole homophily indices towards being more homophilous overall, but it also did so for the Cajun indices.
      This is because one of the two participants who did not provide network data, Errol, identified as a Cajun.
      The mean homophily index was assigned to those who had no network data for all the analyses here, and that mean had shifted to being more negative, shifting the Cajun boxplot in turn.
      However, the greater impact was obviously in the range of homophily values for Creoles, who shifted from seemingly being quite spread out to being, probably more accurately, as ethnically homophilous as Cajuns.

      Additionally, when looking at the adjusted boxplots for Creoles and Cajuns, the level of ethnic homophily for Cajuns was similar to the racial homophily of White respondents to the GSS in the region, although the ethnic homophily of Creoles was high but not as extreme as the racial homophily of Black respondents to the GSS.
      The White population of the region had a mean racial homophily of \Sexpr{Round(gssHomophilyByRace["Mean", "White"])} ($SD \approx$ \Sexpr{Round(gssHomophilyByRace["SD", "White"])}, $N$ = \Sexpr{gssHomophilyByRace["N", "White"]}), and the Black population in the region had a mean racial homophily of \Sexpr{Round(gssHomophilyByRace["Mean", "Black"])} ($SD \approx$ \Sexpr{Round(gssHomophilyByRace["SD", "Black"])}, $N$ = \Sexpr{gssHomophilyByRace["N", "Black"]}).
      The networks are more congruent when looking at exactly who was included in the personal networks of GSS respondents.
      White respondents named a total of \Sexpr{nrow(gss[gss$Ethnicity == "White" & gss$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Black",])} Black alters out of \Sexpr{nrow(gss[gss$Ethnicity == "White",])} alters, and Black respondents named a total of \Sexpr{nrow(gss[gss$Ethnicity == "Black" & gss$`Alter Ethnicity` == "White",])} White alters out of \Sexpr{nrow(gss[gss$Ethnicity == "Black",])} alters.
      Where respondents had more diverse networks, it was almost entirely the result of White respondents including Hispanic alters in their networks, these alters numbering \Sexpr{nrow(gss[gss$Ethnicity == "White" & gss$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Hispanic",])}.
      The same sort of near complete disconnect between Black and White GSS respondents is also present in the networks of Creole and Cajun participants here in that, if one were to exclude all alters that were not Black or White in the former case and all alters that were not Creole or Cajun in the latter case, both datasets would yield almost perfectly homophilous networks.
      Such a drastic disconnect between Creoles and Cajuns in the present study likely helps explain the lack of statistical significance in the modeling results that will be discussed in the next section.

    \subsection{Personal networks' impact on subject pronoun use}
      \label{sec:network_results_pronouns}
      The personal social network data for the participants in this study presents evidence that Creole participants generally do not form relationships with Cajuns and Cajun participants generally do not form relationships with Creoles.
      Personal networks that were not ethnically homophilous did not reach that state due to a mix of Creole and Cajun alters, meaning no indications of a shortening of social distance between the groups were found.
      This would suggest that there are limited vectors for linguistic diffusion between these groups, although such diffusion cannot be completely ruled out \foreign{a priori}.
      To this end, a fine-grained examination of whether the degree of ethnic homophily in personal networks is predictive of subject pronoun use is warranted.
      In other words, the question still remains as to whether those Creoles with less homophilous networks than other Creoles were more likely to use non-Creole-marked pronouns than Creoles with more homophilous networks and \foreign{vice versa} for Cajuns.
      The following aims to quantitatively shed light on the answer to that question, the primary question of this chapter, by fitting the model in Equation \ref{eq:regression_model_homophily} for each pronoun type.

      \begin{enumerate}[label=\roman*., ref=\roman*, resume=equations]
        \item Pronoun Type $\sim$ Network Ethnic Homophily * Ethnicity + (1|Speaker) + (1|Predicate)\label{eq:regression_model_homophily}
      \end{enumerate}
      
      The results indicate that the degree of ethnic homophily does not predict subject pronoun realizations.
      This result is summed up in Table \ref{tab:homophily_results}, and the raw output for all of the models can be seen in Appendix \ref{app:code_homophily}.
      No factors were significant for any pronoun type.
      This is perhaps expected given the lack of evidence for vectors of diffusion between Creoles and Cajuns just discussed.
      To the extent that Creoles are the users of Creole-marked subject pronouns and Cajuns are the users of Cajun-marked subject pronouns, exposure that could lead to acquisition of the pronouns of the other group would require some level of interaction with them rather than with simply any member of any out-group.

