                       & \multicolumn{2}{c}{}       & Cajun     & \Sexpr{Round(logitModelHomophilySummaries$expletive$coefficients["il~EthnicityCajun", "Estimate"] + logitModelHomophilySummaries$expletive$coefficients["il~(Intercept)", "Estimate"])}   & \Sexpr{Round(logitModelHomophilySummaries$expletive$coefficients["il~`Network Ethnic Homophily`:EthnicityCajun", "Estimate"])}  & \Sexpr{Ceiling(logitModelHomophilySummaries$expletive$coefficients["il~`Network Ethnic Homophily`:EthnicityCajun", "Pr(>|z|)"])}  & \\
          impersonal   & tu   & to                  & Creole    & \Sexpr{Round(logitModelHomophilySummaries$impersonal$coefficients["to~(Intercept)", "Estimate"])}                                                                                         & \Sexpr{Round(logitModelHomophilySummaries$impersonal$coefficients["to~`Network Ethnic Homophily`", "Estimate"])}                & \Sexpr{Ceiling(logitModelHomophilySummaries$impersonal$coefficients["to~`Network Ethnic Homophily`", "Pr(>|z|)"])}                & \\
                       & \multicolumn{2}{c}{}       & Cajun     & \Sexpr{Round(logitModelHomophilySummaries$impersonal$coefficients["to~EthnicityCajun", "Estimate"] + logitModelHomophilySummaries$impersonal$coefficients["to~(Intercept)", "Estimate"])} & \Sexpr{Round(logitModelHomophilySummaries$impersonal$coefficients["to~`Network Ethnic Homophily`:EthnicityCajun", "Estimate"])} & \Sexpr{Ceiling(logitModelHomophilySummaries$impersonal$coefficients["to~`Network Ethnic Homophily`:EthnicityCajun", "Pr(>|z|)"])} & \\
        \end{tabular}
      \end{table}

      Creoles are, however, not the sole users of Creole-marked pronouns and Cajuns are not the sole users of Cajun-marked pronouns.
      There are several possible implications for this.
      The first is that alters who were not Creole nor Cajun have acted as the vectors for the diffusion of these pronouns.
      A number of these alters who spoke F\&C with participants were European (\Sexpr{nrow(networks[networks$`Alter French Frequency` != "Never" & networks$`Alter Ethnicity` == "French", ])} French, \Sexpr{nrow(networks[networks$`Alter French Frequency` != "Never" & networks$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Belgian", ])} Belgian) or Canadian (\Sexpr{nrow(networks[networks$`Alter French Frequency` != "Never" & networks$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Quebecois", ])} Quebecois, \Sexpr{nrow(networks[networks$`Alter French Frequency` != "Never" & networks$`Alter Ethnicity` == "Acadian", ])} Acadian), none of whom would be plausible users of Creole-marked pronouns although they may use a number of Cajun-marked pronouns.
      The other larger group of these alters were American ($N$ = \Sexpr{nrow(networks[networks$`Alter French Frequency` != "Never" & networks$`Alter Ethnicity` == "American", ])}), whose speech patterns cannot be ascertained at all.
      Perhaps these Americans are a mix of Creole- and Cajun-marked pronoun users, which would fit into the second possible implication, that being that some unobserved vectors of diffusion do in fact exist between Creoles and Cajuns.
      They may be unobserved in the way that American alters' speech patterns are not known here or in that alters exist who were not elicited through the name generator questions used for this study.
      This possibility cannot be addressed here, unfortunately.
      The last and arguably most likely implication, though, is that intra-group diffusion occurs either synchronically or diachronically or both.
      In the diachronic case, one could posit that the source of Cajun-marked pronouns as used by Creoles and \foreign{vice versa} is not recent at all and is instead linked to a past social setting whose remnants are now more observable via the examination of a social factor other than ethnicity.
      The ensuing examination of speakers who are outliers to the overall subject pronoun pattern will suggest place as perhaps that social factor.

      Participants whose subject pronoun systems did not follow the more general pattern are summarized in Table \ref{tab:contradictory_systems} where the pronoun system column describes the attested patterns, either Cajuns who used Creole-marked pronouns exclusively, Creoles who used Cajun-marked pronouns exclusively, speakers who used a mix of Creole- and Cajun-marked pronouns, and speakers who switched or claimed to be able to switch between Creole- and Cajun-marked pronoun systems as a whole.
      Those who mixed pronoun systems into one new system were distinguished from those who switched between two systems completely by the latter speakers producing different pronouns for several pronoun types, although this does not preclude the possibility that the former were not ultimately switchers who simply did not demonstrate such linguistic behavior during their interviews.

