      The Cajuns who used exclusively Creole-marked pronouns were Elizabeth and Errol.
      In terms of ethnic homophily, both had primarily Cajun personal networks whether going by the original calculated values or the adjusted values shown in Figure \ref{fig:homophily_exploratory_boxplots}, whereas if homophily was the explanation for their linguistic behavior, primarily Creole alters might instead be expected in their networks.
      Their self-identifications as White also go no farther towards offering insights into their speech as almost every other Cajun also identified as White.
      What perhaps has more explanatory power is the fact that Elizabeth had lived her whole life in Henderson and Errol in Breaux Bridge.
      Not only are these towns in St Martin Parish where Creole is commonly said to be spoken, but they are some of the exact towns in that parish that have been cited as having a high proportion of Creole speakers \parencite[p.~40]{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}.
      It should be noted, however, that Elizabeth produced \convertSmallNumber{\Sexpr{tablesParticipant$firstSg$`Elizabeth Naquin`["je"]}} tokens of Cajun-marked \lexi{je} at the very beginning of her interview, so it is possible that she had the ability to switch between pronoun systems despite not doing so otherwise.
      Similarly, Errol produced two tokens of \lexi{je} initially but also used a token of \lexi{je} much later to seemingly take a stance on a topic.
      He, too, was quite possibly capable of using Cajun-marked pronouns more generally.
      In Elizabeth's case, this would be in line with her more worldly lifestyle, relative to others in the community, that would bring her into contact with people from a variety of walks of life, including leading tours to various locations around the country.\footnote{
        The interview schedule included questions about activities such as family vacations, and many participants explained that they only rarely vacationed if at all and/or did not vacation very far from home.
      } For Errol, the source of his ability to additionally use Cajun-marked pronouns is not clear, but his reasons for doing so will be explored more in Chapter \ref{chap:discourse}, as will Elizabeth's.

      In flipping this pattern, there were five Creoles who exlusively used Cajun-marked pronouns: James, Samantha, Terry, Jerry, and Deborah.
      Once again, their ethnic homophily scores were not instructive in explaining their linguistic behavior.
      Samantha and Terry both had primarily Creole personal networks, the opposite of what one might expect for those who use Cajun-marked pronouns if ethnic homophily were predictive.
      James' personal network was in fact on the more diverse side, but as previously discussed, this was not exactly due to the presence of Cajuns in his network nor to the absence of Creoles.
      Creole and African were in fact the ethnicities he assigned to his alters the most frequently, whereas he described none of his alters explicitly as Cajun.
      One of James' alters was assigned White as an ethnicity, however, that alter also being a participant who self-identified as Cajun: Richard.
      The influence of Cajun-marked speech through Cajuns in James' network cannot be completely ruled out, although as will be discussed farther below, he did not appear to be targeting Cajun-like speech exactly.

      Two of the five Creoles who used Cajun-marked pronounces exclusively, Deborah and Jerry, presented slightly different considerations.
      Neither had Cajuns in their networks, although Jerry's network was half Creole and half non-Creole whereas Deborah's was also absent of Creoles.
      Given this difference in ethnic homophily, and given that both participants self-identified as White Creoles, one might expect to see differences in their speech patterns, but both used exclusively Cajun-marked subject pronouns.
      This suggests again that the degree of ethnic homophily does not have an impact on subject pronouns in a gradient way.

      Both speakers' self-identifications as White are worth considering, though.
      Cajun has become highly, although not explicitly, linked to Whiteness, and White identities were shown in Chapter \ref{chap:pronoun_variation} to seemingly be associated with the same subject pronouns as Cajun identities.
      Unfortunately, participants were not asked to racially describe their alters, and neither used US racialized categories to descirbe alters' ethnicities, so whether either speaker's alters would also be considered by them to be White is not clear.

      Place may also be behind these Creoles' incongruities with the more general subject pronoun tendencies of Creole participants, though.
      Jerry grew up in Mansura, part of Avoyelles Parish where Creole has not been formally attested, and Deborah grew up in the town of Creole, which, despite its name, is a town far to the southwest of Lafayette in a parish where again Creole has not been formally attested.
      Likewise, Terry has spent most of his life between the particularly rural towns of Mallet and Swords which are both almost equally distant from Opelousas, Ville Platte, Eunice, and Church Point.
      Out of these larger towns, only Opelousas has been attested as having Creole speakers in addition to a predominance of French speakers \parencite{klingler_language_2003}.
      James as well grew up in DeQuincy, closer to the Texas border in the west than to parishes like St Martin and St Landry that are closely linked to Creole language.
      Out of these Creoles who used Cajun-marked pronouns, only Samantha grew up in a place where Creole has been said to have a more regular presence, that place being Lafayette.
      Being far younger than the other Creoles, however, may have impacted the sort of exposure she received despite two members of her family speaking F\&C.
      She was clearly more influenced by international varieties of French as her language was a product of the French immersion school system in Louisiana, and she herself described her speech as more European.
      James' speech, likewise, appeared to target an international variety of French rather than targeting the way Cajuns speak, which may be informed by the fact that he claimed to only begin regularly using F\&C when he went to Cameroon to teach as an adult.

      One participant whose subject pronoun system was consistent while being interviewed but included a mix of Creole- and Cajun-marked pronouns was Gene.
      Specifically, he used \lexi{yé} for 3pl and Cajun-marked pronouns for all other pronoun types.
      Given his almost complete rejection of racialized labels for himself and ethnic or racialized labels for his alters, it is difficult to conclude whether his ethnic homophily could be related to this linguistic behavior at all.
      His residence history is quite interesting in regards to his speech, though.
      He essentially spent his life in Carencro just north of Lafayette where the border between the two towns has shifted over time, hence he was also able to say that he lived in Lafayette at the time of the interview.
      Carencro has not been discussed much in the literature, but Creole speech patterns are anecdotally common there, and in fact an individual who was present during Gene's interview suggested that he spoke more Creole-like to others than he did towards myself.
      His mixed system of subject pronouns may thus have been the result of switching towards Cajun-marked pronouns but only imperfectly.
      This imperfection is perhaps explainable by the lack of Cajuns in his network and the ease with which he recounted conflicts that he had had in his life with White Louisianians given that he defined Cajuns plainly as White people.
      In effect, Gene may have felt it necessary to learn to speak like Cajuns but not because of an affinity for them.
      This explanation for his mixed subject pronoun system must admittedly remain speculative, however.

      Others in Table \ref{tab:contradictory_systems} made it clearer that they could switch systematically between Creole-marked and Cajun-marked subject pronouns.
      For Owen, who generally seemed to prefer Creole-marked pronouns, his ethnic homophily values are just as difficult to interpret in relation to his linguistic behavior as Gene's are as his network also ranged anywhere from entirely absent of homophily for the initial calculation to completely homophilous after exploratory recoding of his alters' ethnicities.
      His preference for Creole-marked pronouns, however, is in line with his ethnic and racialized identities as well as the fact that he has spent most of his life in Breaux Bridge.
      The fact that he could use Cajun-marked pronouns appeared to have more to do with his experience with academic French than any possible contacts he had had with Cajuns given that, in these moments, he generally used subject pronouns and verbal inflections, such as \lexi{nous disons} \gloss{we say}, that are not even particularly common in the casual speech of francophones in other parts of the world let alone Cajuns.
      Owen switched between subject pronoun systems much more than any other participants, making his discourse with relation to the pronoun choices embedded in different parts of that discourse more available for qualitative analysis as will be done in the following chapter.

      Oliver L. and Steven, on the other hand, had a much clearer preference for Cajun-marked subject pronouns in the context of interviews.
      Indeed, Oliver L. produced a total of two examples early on in the interview, and Steven only intimated that he could speak Creole as well towards the end of the interview but never produced any examples.
      Both participants are White Cajuns whose personal networks were primarily made up of other Cajuns, making neither their identities nor their networks enlightening for their speech patterns.
      What once again appears to be a more likely explanation for their subject pronoun versatility is the fact that both are originally from towns in St Martin Parish, St Martinville for Oliver L. and Cecilia for Steven.
      Cecilia is a town not only highlighted in the literature as being assoiciated with Creole, but as will be seen in the next chapter, it is specifically associated with White people who speak Creole even for some of the participants of the present study.
      Oliver L. in fact explained that his ability to use Creole was related to being partially raised by a nanny who spoke Creole.
      The reasons for his choices to switch between varieties will be discussed further in the next chapter.

  \section{Discussion}
    In sum, there was not a statistically significant association between the degree of ethnic homophily in participants' networks and the mix of subject pronouns they employed.
    What can be seen in their networks, though, is that Creole participants were not connected to Cajuns and Cajun participants were not connected to Creoles save for the possibility that unobserved alters exist.
    This in turn suggests limited vectors for the diffusion of linguistic features between the two groups, yet there are in fact Creoles who use Cajun-marked subject pronouns and Cajuns who use Creole-marked subject pronouns.
    There are several possible explanations for this, but the one for which the current data provides the most evidence is that the pattern of subject pronoun usage between Creoles and Cajuns came about at some point in the past that is more observable now through a social variable other than ethnicity.
    Perhaps the best candidate for that variable is place as this was repeatedly relevant to the linguistic behavior of speakers who did not follow the overall trends for subject pronouns.
    As will be seen in Chapter \ref{chap:discourse}, place also regularly came up in discourse about ways of speaking F\&C.

    Evidence in linguistic studies has mostly pointed to race being an obstacle to diffusion as being true.
    \textcite{edwards_sociolinguistic_1992}, although looking at interaction between White and Black residents of Detroit to build a centrality index for Black study participants, nonetheless presented results that indicated that Black speakers who did not have White friends used more Black linguistic features in their English.
    This reiteration of the result that racialized boundaries are not often crossed in terms of sharing linguistic features just as when sending literal messages \parencite{korte_acquaintance_1970} is important given that the diffusion of different types of messages or aspects of culture need not be governed by the same factors.
    Likewise, racial homophily in personal networks was found to be significant in \citeauthor{fridland_network_2003}'s (\citeyear{fridland_network_2003}) and \citeauthor{ash_linguistic_1986}'s (\citeyear{ash_linguistic_1986}) studies in Memphis and Philadelphia, respectively.
    The latter study was particularly robust in that it also included participants who had low racial homophily.
    The present study is only in line with these previous results to the extent that the network data suggests a lack of vectors for diffusion between Creoles and Cajuns, but those speakers who contradicted the general tendencies for subject pronouns were not explainable through recourse to their network structures.

    One past linguistic study that looked at the effects of the make-up of racialized identities in networks on language variation showed that there are caveats to the effects of race in networks in that the personal histories of racialized in-group members are also important \parencite{labov_facto_1986}.
    Essentially, the implication was that not all members of a racialized group are the same in terms of experiences that may shape linguistic behavior.
    Similarly, ethnic homophily was found to be predictive of linguistic behavior for Polish immigrants \parencite{schleef_teenagers_2011} and Punjabis \parencite{sharma_cognitive_2011} in the UK but not for all subsets of these groups, again suggesting that there are additional factors that can override potential network effects.
    These mixed results are perhaps relevant to those participants in the present study who did not follow the more general subject pronoun tendencies of Creoles and Cajuns.
    While the impact of other factors for these speakers is speculative and exploratory, place appears to be consistently implicated as important while the importance of broad racialized self-identifications and exposure to institutional French cannot be ruled out either.

    Occasionally, previous linguistic studies have even shown a lack of any effect for racial homophily on language variation \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][]{van_hofwegen_coming_2010}, just as no effect was found for the present study.
    The interactions found for Polish and Punjabi immigrants in the UK that led to mixed results for the effect of homophily may again be instructive on this point.
    A lack of effect for homophily does not mean that homophily cannot be predictive of linguistic behavior but rather that it is not predictive in a particular setting where other factors may be overriding homophily.
    That setting in \textcite{van_hofwegen_coming_2010} was African American speakers in North Carolina, whereas the setting here was Creole and Cajun F\&C-speakers in the modern-day surroundings of Lafayette, Louisiana, and in the present study, the most implicated overriding factor was place.